import React from 'react'
type S={heading:string;text:string[]}; type G={title:string;sections:S[]}
const guides:Record<string,G>={
"cap-rate-calculator":{title:"Cap Rate Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"Cap rate begins with stabilized NOI, not gross rent",text:["Capitalization rate compares a property's annual net operating income with its value or purchase price. The numerator should be a defensible, stabilized NOI: recurring property income minus ordinary operating expenses needed to keep the asset producing that income. Gross scheduled rent by itself is not NOI, and using it will overstate the apparent yield. Vacancy, credit loss, management, repairs, insurance, property taxes, utilities paid by the owner, landscaping and other recurring operating costs normally belong in the analysis.","A useful workflow is to build the income statement first and calculate cap rate second. Separate current in-place performance from a stabilized scenario. If a building is temporarily half empty, current NOI may describe today's economics while stabilized NOI may better describe the asset after lease-up. Keep both cases visible rather than quietly replacing weak current numbers with optimistic assumptions."]},
{heading:"Debt service is deliberately excluded",text:["Cap rate is an unlevered property metric. Mortgage principal and interest are financing choices, so they are not deducted when computing NOI. Two buyers can purchase the same property with different down payments and loan terms while the underlying property's cap rate remains the same. This makes cap rate useful for comparing assets before financing.","After evaluating the property on an unlevered basis, add financing metrics such as cash-on-cash return and debt-service coverage ratio. A high cap rate does not guarantee attractive equity returns if debt is expensive, and a lower-cap-rate property can still fit an investor whose financing, growth expectations and risk tolerance are different."]},
{heading:"Price and cap rate move in opposite directions",text:["For a given NOI, value equals NOI divided by the market cap rate. If investors require a higher cap rate, the indicated value falls; if they accept a lower cap rate, the indicated value rises. This inverse relationship is why modest cap-rate changes can have large effects on property valuation.","Suppose stabilized NOI is $80,000. At a 5 percent cap rate, the implied value is $1.6 million; at 6 percent it is about $1.33 million; at 7 percent it is about $1.14 million. This sensitivity is important when underwriting an exit price several years into a holding period."]},
{heading:"Cap rates reflect risk, growth and market structure",text:["A cap rate is not simply a score where higher is always better. Lower cap rates often appear in locations or property types where investors perceive stronger demand, better liquidity, lower risk or better long-term growth. Higher cap rates may compensate for tenant concentration, weak locations, deferred maintenance, volatile demand or limited buyer depth.","Compare like with like. A suburban single-tenant retail property, an urban apartment building and an industrial warehouse can trade at different cap rates for legitimate reasons. Market evidence should be segmented by geography, asset class, quality, lease structure, tenant profile and transaction date."]},
{heading:"Normalize unusual income and expenses",text:["One-time insurance proceeds, temporary concessions, owner-specific expenses and deferred repairs can distort a single year's NOI. Underwriting should identify items that will not persist and recurring costs that are currently understated. Management expense deserves particular attention when an owner self-manages for free; a buyer may still need to pay for that function.","Capital expenditures are another nuance. Traditional NOI commonly excludes major capital replacements, but roofs, HVAC systems and unit renovations still consume cash. Investors often maintain a separate reserve or calculate an adjusted yield after recurring capital needs. State clearly which convention your model uses."]},
{heading:"Use trailing and forward NOI carefully",text:["Trailing-12-month NOI is grounded in actual operations, while forward NOI incorporates signed leases, known rent steps and expected expenses. Neither is automatically superior. A rapidly improving property can look unattractive on trailing figures, while an aggressive forward forecast can make a risky acquisition appear deceptively cheap.","A strong analysis displays both. Reconcile the difference line by line: occupancy, rent growth, concessions, taxes, insurance and other expenses. If most of the investment thesis depends on future NOI that has not yet been achieved, the uncertainty should be reflected in the required return."]},
{heading:"Exit-cap assumptions can dominate a value-add model",text:["Value-add underwriting frequently assumes NOI growth plus a resale at an exit cap rate. Using an exit cap lower than today's acquisition cap embeds multiple expansion and can create a large paper gain even without exceptional operating improvement. That assumption deserves explicit justification.","Stress test the exit cap at several levels, including a case above the entry cap. Interest rates, capital availability, local supply and investor sentiment can all change during a multi-year hold. The purpose of sensitivity analysis is not to predict one exact future cap rate but to reveal how dependent the projected return is on resale pricing."]},
{heading:"Cap rate should be one part of due diligence",text:["Cap rate says little about lease expirations, physical condition, environmental issues, zoning, title, tenant credit, neighborhood supply or future capital projects. Two properties with identical cap rates can have radically different risk. Treat the metric as a compact summary of price relative to NOI, not as a complete investment decision.","Before committing capital, reconcile rent rolls to leases, inspect operating statements, verify taxes and insurance, review major contracts and inspect the property. For commercial assets, tenant rollover and leasing commissions can be especially important. For residential rentals, local regulation, turnover and maintenance history can materially affect realized returns."]},
{heading:"Compare cap rate with cash-on-cash return and IRR",text:["Cash-on-cash return focuses on annual pre-tax cash flow relative to equity invested, so it incorporates financing. Internal rate of return can incorporate multiple years of cash flows and a resale value. Cap rate is simpler and useful for property-level comparison, but it does not replace these equity-return measures.","Use all three when appropriate. Cap rate asks what yield the property produces before debt; cash-on-cash asks what current cash yield the equity receives; IRR asks what discount rate equates the entire modeled stream of contributions and distributions."]},
{heading:"Build a sensitivity table before relying on one answer",text:["Small changes in rent, vacancy, taxes and required cap rate can materially change indicated value. Create downside, base and upside cases rather than reporting a single number to several decimal places. Precision in arithmetic is not precision in assumptions.","A useful stress case combines lower occupancy with higher expenses and a higher exit cap. If the investment only works when every assumption is favorable, the headline cap rate is giving an incomplete picture of risk."]},
{heading:"A worked example makes the calculation easier to audit",text:["When using the Cap Rate Calculator, write the inputs down before looking at the result and independently check the units and direction of the calculation. A worked example is most useful when it shows not only the arithmetic but also why each input belongs in the formula. Change one assumption at a time and observe which output moves. That process helps distinguish a sensitive input from one that has little effect in the current scenario.","Do not copy an example's assumptions into a real decision merely because the numbers look similar. Examples simplify reality. Replace every example value with information that matches the actual property, tax situation, investment, employment arrangement or clinical measurement involved, and preserve the source of important inputs so the result can be reviewed later."]},
{heading:"Interpret ranges rather than false precision",text:["The Cap Rate Calculator can calculate precise arithmetic, but many inputs are estimates or measurements with uncertainty. Reporting many decimal places can imply more certainty than the underlying data supports. Round the final result to a level that is meaningful for the decision and use scenarios when assumptions can reasonably vary.","Sensitivity analysis is especially valuable when a small input change produces a large change in the conclusion. In those cases, the correct takeaway may be that the decision is assumption-sensitive rather than that one scenario is definitively right."]},
{heading:"Why can two brokers quote different cap rates for the same property?",text:["They may be using different NOI periods, expense assumptions or price definitions. One may use trailing NOI while another uses a pro forma; one may include management expense while another omits it. Ask for the exact numerator and denominator before comparing quoted cap rates.","Recalculate from the underlying operating statement whenever possible. A cap rate is only comparable when the accounting conventions are comparable."]},
{heading:"How should vacancy be modeled?",text:["Use an assumption consistent with the property's actual leases and market conditions. A fully occupied building should not automatically be underwritten at zero vacancy forever, while a distressed building should not automatically be valued as though immediate full occupancy were certain.","Consider physical vacancy, economic vacancy, concessions and bad debt separately when they are material. Stabilized vacancy should be supported by market evidence rather than chosen solely to reach a target value."]},
{heading:"What happens when property taxes reset after a sale?",text:["In some jurisdictions a transfer or reassessment can change the tax bill materially. Using the seller's historical property tax expense may therefore overstate the buyer's future NOI.","Estimate taxes under the rules applicable after acquisition and document the assumption. The same principle applies to insurance quotes and utilities when current expenses are unlikely to continue."]},
{heading:"Can a negative cap rate occur?",text:["Yes. If operating expenses exceed property income, NOI is negative and the mathematical cap rate is negative. That does not make the formula invalid; it signals that the asset is currently losing money before financing.","For turnaround properties, focus on the path from current to stabilized NOI, the capital required to get there and the probability of achieving the plan rather than treating a negative current cap rate as a conventional yield."]},
]},
"ankle-brachial-index-calculator":{title:"Ankle-Brachial Index Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"What the ABI compares",text:["The ankle-brachial index, or ABI, is a ratio comparing systolic blood pressure measured at the ankle with systolic pressure measured in the arm. Clinicians use it as a noninvasive part of evaluating blood flow to the legs and possible peripheral artery disease. The arithmetic is simple, but obtaining reliable pressures and interpreting the result in clinical context require care.","An online calculator can perform the ratio once the measurements are available; it cannot establish why a pressure is abnormal. Symptoms, pulse examination, cardiovascular risk, vascular calcification and testing technique all influence what a result means."]},
{heading:"Measurement technique affects the number",text:["ABI testing is commonly performed after a period of rest with the patient lying supine. Pressures are measured in both arms and at ankle arteries, often using a blood-pressure cuff and Doppler device. Cuff size, patient position, recent activity and operator technique can change readings.","For each leg, clinical protocols generally use an ankle systolic pressure and an appropriate brachial reference pressure. Because protocols and reporting conventions can differ, enter measurements from the clinician or laboratory exactly as instructed rather than reconstructing a medical test from casual home readings."]},
{heading:"How ratios are generally interpreted",text:["ABI values around the normal range suggest that ankle systolic pressure is not substantially reduced relative to arm pressure. Lower ratios can be associated with arterial obstruction, while unusually high or noncompressible readings can occur when arteries are stiff or calcified and the cuff cannot compress them normally.","Interpretive cutoffs are clinical conventions rather than universal diagnoses. A borderline result may need correlation with symptoms or additional testing, and a seemingly normal resting ABI does not rule out every vascular problem. The report from the treating clinician or vascular laboratory should take priority over a generic calculator category."]},
{heading:"A high ABI is not automatically reassuring",text:["Very high ankle pressures can make the calculated ratio look excellent even when the measurement is unreliable. Medial arterial calcification, seen more often in some people with diabetes, kidney disease or advanced age, can make vessels difficult to compress. In that setting, clinicians may use tests such as toe-brachial index or other vascular studies.","This illustrates an important limitation of ratio calculators: mathematically larger is not always physiologically better. When a result falls outside the measurable or expected range, the next question is whether the underlying pressure measurement is valid."]},
{heading:"Symptoms matter even when a number looks normal",text:["Leg discomfort with walking, wounds that heal poorly, rest pain, temperature or color changes and diminished pulses can prompt vascular evaluation. Conversely, some people with peripheral artery disease have few obvious symptoms. ABI is therefore one piece of an assessment rather than a substitute for history and examination.","If a clinician ordered ABI because of symptoms, discuss discordance between the result and symptoms rather than assuming the calculator settles the question. Exercise ABI or imaging may sometimes be considered when resting measurements do not explain the clinical picture."]},
{heading:"Right and left legs should be considered separately",text:["Peripheral arterial disease can affect one leg more than the other. Calculating a single blended ABI can hide asymmetry, so clinical reports generally preserve right- and left-leg results. Review the individual pressures as well as the final ratios.","Large differences between arms can also be clinically relevant and may affect which brachial pressure is used as the denominator under a specific protocol. This is another reason the measurement process should follow a standardized clinical method."]},
{heading:"ABI is linked to broader cardiovascular risk",text:["Peripheral artery disease is a manifestation of systemic atherosclerotic disease and can be associated with cardiovascular risk beyond the legs. An abnormal ABI may therefore lead clinicians to consider the patient's overall cardiovascular risk profile, not merely local leg symptoms.","A calculator should not recommend medication or treatment from the ratio alone. Decisions about exercise programs, antiplatelet therapy, lipid management, smoking cessation, imaging or procedures depend on diagnosis, symptoms, bleeding risk, comorbidities and professional assessment."]},
{heading:"Use this calculator as an arithmetic aid",text:["The safest role for this tool is to calculate and explain the ratio from measurements obtained appropriately. It is educational and cannot diagnose peripheral artery disease, determine urgency or replace a vascular examination. Do not delay medical evaluation because a calculated category appears normal.","Seek prompt professional assessment for concerning new symptoms, especially severe limb pain, a cold or pale limb, rapidly worsening wounds or other acute changes. For routine questions, take the measurements and calculator result to a qualified healthcare professional who can interpret them in context."]},
{heading:"Exercise testing can reveal problems missed at rest",text:["Some patients have exertional leg symptoms despite a resting ABI that is not clearly abnormal. In selected clinical settings, supervised exercise testing with repeat ankle pressures can help evaluate whether blood flow falls after activity.","This requires an appropriate clinical protocol and is not equivalent to taking a blood-pressure reading after an ordinary walk at home. The timing, equipment and interpretation matter."]},
{heading:"Track source measurements, not just the ratio",text:["When reviewing serial ABI results, keep the actual arm and ankle systolic pressures, the side tested, date and method. A ratio can change because the numerator changed, the denominator changed or both changed.","Trend interpretation should account for measurement variability and clinical status. A small numerical difference does not necessarily represent meaningful disease progression or improvement."]},
{heading:"A worked example makes the calculation easier to audit",text:["When using the Ankle-Brachial Index Calculator, write the inputs down before looking at the result and independently check the units and direction of the calculation. A worked example is most useful when it shows not only the arithmetic but also why each input belongs in the formula. Change one assumption at a time and observe which output moves. That process helps distinguish a sensitive input from one that has little effect in the current scenario.","Do not copy an example's assumptions into a real decision merely because the numbers look similar. Examples simplify reality. Replace every example value with information that matches the actual property, tax situation, investment, employment arrangement or clinical measurement involved, and preserve the source of important inputs so the result can be reviewed later."]},
{heading:"Interpret ranges rather than false precision",text:["The Ankle-Brachial Index Calculator can calculate precise arithmetic, but many inputs are estimates or measurements with uncertainty. Reporting many decimal places can imply more certainty than the underlying data supports. Round the final result to a level that is meaningful for the decision and use scenarios when assumptions can reasonably vary.","Sensitivity analysis is especially valuable when a small input change produces a large change in the conclusion. In those cases, the correct takeaway may be that the decision is assumption-sensitive rather than that one scenario is definitively right."]},
{heading:"Why are Doppler measurements commonly used?",text:["At the ankle, pulse sounds may be easier to detect reliably with Doppler equipment than with ordinary auscultation. The cuff is inflated and released while flow is detected, allowing systolic pressure to be estimated according to the testing protocol.","Equipment and operator training matter. An online form can calculate a ratio but cannot improve an inaccurately obtained pressure."]},
{heading:"Can edema or wounds affect testing?",text:["Leg wounds, severe edema, pain, recent procedures and other local factors can complicate cuff placement or make a standard test inappropriate. Clinical staff may modify the approach or choose another test based on the situation.","Do not place cuffs over areas where a clinician has advised against compression. Testing decisions should follow professional instructions."]},
{heading:"Why might toe pressures be used?",text:["Digital arteries can sometimes remain compressible when larger ankle arteries are stiff from calcification. Toe pressure or toe-brachial index can therefore provide useful information in selected patients with noncompressible ankle measurements.","Toe testing has its own technique and interpretation. Do not substitute an ankle ratio formula for a toe-brachial protocol."]},
{heading:"Does ABI measure venous disease?",text:["ABI primarily evaluates arterial pressure relationships. Symptoms caused by venous insufficiency, neuropathy, musculoskeletal disease or other conditions are not diagnosed by this ratio.","A normal ABI therefore does not mean every cause of leg pain or swelling has been excluded. Clinical evaluation should be directed by the symptoms and examination."]},
]},
"w2-vs-1099-calculator":{title:"W-2 vs 1099 Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"Compare total compensation, not headline pay",text:["A W-2 salary and a 1099 contract rate are not economically equivalent. Employees may receive employer-paid payroll taxes, health insurance, retirement contributions, paid leave, equipment, training and unemployment or workers' compensation protections. Independent contractors generally need to price many of those costs into their rate.","Start with annualized cash compensation, then create separate rows for benefits, taxes, unpaid time and business expenses. This prevents a common mistake: comparing a $100,000 salary directly with $100,000 of contractor revenue and assuming the two offers are equal."]},
{heading:"Payroll tax treatment is materially different",text:["Employees generally split Social Security and Medicare payroll taxes with their employer through FICA withholding. A self-employed worker generally bears both the employee and employer portions through self-employment tax, subject to applicable rules, thresholds and deductions. Income tax is separate from payroll tax.","Contractors may be able to deduct qualifying business expenses and generally receive an income-tax deduction for the employer-equivalent portion of self-employment tax, but those provisions do not make the extra payroll-tax burden disappear. Model the components explicitly rather than applying one guessed tax percentage."]},
{heading:"Benefits need a realistic replacement cost",text:["Employer health coverage can be worth far more than the employee's visible payroll deduction because the employer may subsidize a large portion of the premium. Retirement matches, disability coverage, life insurance and other benefits also have economic value. Use the employer contribution or realistic replacement cost when possible.","Not every benefit should be valued at its sticker price. A perk you would never purchase independently may be worth less to you. Build a conservative and a full-value scenario to see whether the decision changes."]},
{heading:"Paid time off changes the contractor hourly rate",text:["Employees may be paid during vacations, holidays and sick days. Contractors usually earn only while billing unless the contract says otherwise. If you expect to take six nonbilling weeks each year, a nominal hourly rate multiplied by 2,080 hours will overstate annual revenue.","Estimate billable weeks, billable hours per week and utilization. Also allow time for administration, proposals, invoicing, bookkeeping and professional development. A contractor rate must support both productive client work and the unpaid work required to run the business."]},
{heading:"Business expenses can be both cost and deduction",text:["Software, hardware, professional insurance, accounting, workspace, licenses and travel may become contractor expenses. A legitimate deduction can reduce taxable business income, but spending one dollar merely to save a fraction of a dollar in tax still reduces cash. Treat the expense first as an economic cost and then separately model its tax effect.","Deductibility depends on facts and tax rules. Personal expenses do not become deductible merely because someone receives a 1099. Keep records and consult current guidance or a tax professional for material or ambiguous items."]},
{heading:"Retirement options differ rather than simply disappearing",text:["Leaving W-2 employment can mean losing an employer match, but self-employed people may have access to arrangements such as a SEP IRA or an individual 401(k), subject to eligibility and contribution rules. The ability to make contributions does not mean the client funds them; the contractor must generate the cash.","When comparing offers, include both the lost employer contribution and the amount you intend to contribute yourself. Retirement-plan administration, deadlines and interaction with other employment can matter for people who have both W-2 and self-employment income."]},
{heading:"Cash-flow timing becomes more important on 1099 income",text:["Employees typically have taxes withheld each paycheck. Contractors often need to reserve cash and make estimated tax payments during the year. Revenue can also arrive irregularly, and clients may pay invoices weeks after work is completed.","A contractor emergency fund often needs to cover business volatility as well as personal expenses. Model slow-paying clients, gaps between contracts and a reasonable bad-debt assumption. A higher expected annual income may still feel financially tighter if cash arrives unpredictably."]},
{heading:"Worker classification is a legal question",text:["A company and worker cannot always choose contractor status solely because it produces a preferred tax result. Classification depends on applicable federal and state tests concerning control, independence and the relationship. Misclassification can create tax, wage and benefit consequences.","Use this calculator to compare legitimate employment and contracting opportunities, not to determine legal status. If the working arrangement is effectively controlled like employment, obtain professional guidance about classification."]},
{heading:"Find the contractor break-even rate",text:["A practical output is the contractor rate required to match a W-2 package. Add salary, employer payroll-tax value, benefits and expected business overhead; then divide by realistic billable hours and adjust for contractor tax treatment. Run multiple cases because health insurance, utilization and tax brackets can dominate the answer.","Finally consider nonfinancial factors: autonomy, schedule control, job security, career development, intellectual property terms and concentration in one client. The calculator can quantify money, but the best choice may depend on risks and preferences that do not fit neatly into a dollar figure."]},
{heading:"Insurance and liability can change the economics",text:["Depending on the work, an independent professional may need general liability, professional liability, cyber coverage, commercial auto insurance or other protection. Contract terms can also shift indemnity or intellectual-property risk to the contractor.","Read the agreement before treating the quoted rate as pure compensation. A rate that looks attractive can be inadequate if the contractor must carry expensive coverage, purchase equipment or accept unusually broad liability."]},
{heading:"Run a hybrid scenario when income comes from both sources",text:["Many people have a W-2 job and side-business income in the same year. Payroll taxes, retirement contribution limits and estimated payments can interact across the two streams. A pure W-2-versus-1099 comparison may not capture those interactions.","Model the marginal effect of the contract on top of existing wages and benefits. The relevant tax rate for additional income may be different from the average rate shown on the existing paycheck."]},
{heading:"A worked example makes the calculation easier to audit",text:["When using the W-2 vs 1099 Calculator, write the inputs down before looking at the result and independently check the units and direction of the calculation. A worked example is most useful when it shows not only the arithmetic but also why each input belongs in the formula. Change one assumption at a time and observe which output moves. That process helps distinguish a sensitive input from one that has little effect in the current scenario.","Do not copy an example's assumptions into a real decision merely because the numbers look similar. Examples simplify reality. Replace every example value with information that matches the actual property, tax situation, investment, employment arrangement or clinical measurement involved, and preserve the source of important inputs so the result can be reviewed later."]},
{heading:"Interpret ranges rather than false precision",text:["The W-2 vs 1099 Calculator can calculate precise arithmetic, but many inputs are estimates or measurements with uncertainty. Reporting many decimal places can imply more certainty than the underlying data supports. Round the final result to a level that is meaningful for the decision and use scenarios when assumptions can reasonably vary.","Sensitivity analysis is especially valuable when a small input change produces a large change in the conclusion. In those cases, the correct takeaway may be that the decision is assumption-sensitive rather than that one scenario is definitively right."]},
{heading:"How much higher should a contractor rate be?",text:["There is no universal multiplier. The required premium depends on benefits, payroll taxes, unpaid time, utilization, insurance, equipment, administrative costs and risk between engagements. A person with expensive family health coverage may need a very different premium from someone covered through a spouse.","Calculate the break-even rate from actual costs, then add a risk margin if appropriate. Rules of thumb can be a starting point but should not replace a personal compensation model."]},
{heading:"Should a contractor compare revenue with gross salary or take-home pay?",text:["Start with gross economics on both sides, then calculate taxes consistently. Comparing contractor revenue with employee take-home pay mixes pre-tax and after-tax figures and can exaggerate the attractiveness of contracting.","A clean model moves from gross compensation to deductible costs, payroll/self-employment taxes, income taxes and finally spendable cash."]},
{heading:"What about bonuses and equity compensation?",text:["Expected bonuses, commissions, restricted stock, options and employee stock purchase benefits can materially change a W-2 package. Use probability-weighted or scenario values when compensation is uncertain rather than assuming the maximum award.","For equity, vesting conditions and liquidity matter. A grant's headline value may not equal cash compensation available today."]},
{heading:"How should bench time be handled?",text:["Contractors can experience periods without billable work between projects. Estimate utilization over a full year rather than assuming every working hour will be invoiced.","A downside scenario with a longer gap is useful for deciding how much cash reserve is needed and whether the quoted rate adequately compensates for volatility."]},
]},
"gold-vs-stocks-calculator":{title:"Gold vs Stocks Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"Gold and stocks produce returns in different ways",text:["Stocks represent ownership in businesses that can generate earnings, reinvest capital and distribute dividends. Gold is a scarce physical asset that does not generate operating cash flow. Its return comes primarily from changes in market price, net of storage, fund fees or trading costs depending on how it is held.","Because the return engines differ, comparing only one recent period can be misleading. A strong decade for one asset does not establish a permanent expected return. Use long horizons and several start dates when exploring historical scenarios."]},
{heading:"Nominal returns are not purchasing-power returns",text:["Inflation changes what a future portfolio can buy. A nominal gain of 5 percent with 4 percent inflation is very different from a 5 percent gain with 1 percent inflation. Compare gold and stocks in both nominal and inflation-adjusted terms when the goal is long-term purchasing power.","Gold is often described as an inflation hedge, but its relationship with inflation can vary considerably over shorter horizons. Stocks also face inflation through costs, interest rates and valuation changes, while some businesses can raise prices. Avoid treating either asset as mechanically linked to CPI."]},
{heading:"Dividends matter in stock comparisons",text:["Price-only stock indexes omit cash dividends, which have historically been an important part of total equity return. A fair long-term comparison should use total-return data with dividends reinvested when evaluating a stock portfolio against gold.","Likewise, include costs on both sides. Gold ETFs may charge expense ratios, physical bullion can involve dealer spreads and storage, and stock funds also have expenses. Taxes can further change after-tax results depending on account type, holding period and jurisdiction."]},
{heading:"Volatility and drawdowns reveal a different dimension",text:["Average return alone does not describe the investor experience. Two assets can have similar compound returns with very different year-to-year volatility and maximum drawdowns. Large losses are especially consequential when an investor must withdraw money during the decline.","Review worst periods and recovery times in addition to ending wealth. A diversified portfolio can combine assets precisely because their returns do not move identically in every environment, although correlations themselves can change."]},
{heading:"Sequence of returns matters for withdrawals",text:["For an investor making regular withdrawals, the order of gains and losses can materially affect portfolio survival. A severe decline early in retirement can force sales at depressed prices even if the long-run average return eventually looks respectable.","A simple lump-sum comparison cannot capture this fully. If the calculator supports contributions or withdrawals, test several return sequences. Otherwise, treat the output as an accumulation illustration rather than a retirement-income forecast."]},
{heading:"Allocation is different from making an all-or-nothing bet",text:["The practical question is often not whether gold or stocks will win, but whether a modest allocation to gold changes the risk profile of a stock-heavy portfolio. Diversification benefits depend on correlation, volatility, rebalancing and the period studied.","Test allocations such as 100/0, 90/10 and 80/20 rather than assuming the decision must be binary. Rebalancing can cause an investor to sell some of the outperforming asset and buy the lagging asset, producing results different from two untouched standalone holdings."]},
{heading:"Starting valuation and macro conditions can influence outcomes",text:["Equity valuations, real interest rates, currency conditions and investor risk appetite can affect future returns. Gold often responds to real yields and safe-haven demand, while stocks respond to earnings expectations, discount rates and economic growth. These relationships are not stable enough to turn into certain forecasts.","Scenario analysis is more defensible than declaring one asset superior from today's conditions. Model conservative, base and optimistic return assumptions for each asset and see which conclusions remain robust."]},
{heading:"Taxes depend on the vehicle and account",text:["Physical precious metals, certain gold funds and ordinary stock investments can receive different tax treatment. Retirement accounts can alter the timing or character of taxes. The details are jurisdiction-specific and can change.","For a meaningful after-tax comparison, identify the actual investment vehicle rather than entering a generic gold return. A low-cost ETF, allocated bullion and collectible coins can have different fees, spreads, custody considerations and tax consequences."]},
{heading:"Historical calculators are educational, not forecasts",text:["Backtests are sensitive to the selected start date, data series, reinvestment assumptions, inflation measure and fees. They are useful for understanding behavior across environments but cannot tell you which asset will outperform next year or over your exact investment horizon.","Use the calculator to test assumptions and understand tradeoffs. Investment decisions should also reflect time horizon, liquidity needs, risk capacity, diversification, taxes and the possibility that future market relationships differ from historical ones."]},
{heading:"Currency denomination can affect the comparison",text:["Gold is globally traded, so returns experienced by an investor can reflect both changes in the metal's global price and changes in the investor's home currency. International stock exposure can also introduce currency effects.","Use data denominated consistently. Mixing a dollar gold series with a stock series reported in another currency can create a comparison that looks precise but answers the wrong question."]},
{heading:"Define the investment objective before selecting the winner",text:["An investor saving for long-term growth may judge success differently from someone prioritizing crisis diversification or near-term capital preservation. No single return statistic captures every objective.","Choose metrics that match the purpose: compound growth for accumulation, drawdown and volatility for risk, real return for purchasing power, and portfolio-level behavior for diversification. Then interpret the calculator in that framework."]},
{heading:"A worked example makes the calculation easier to audit",text:["When using the Gold vs Stocks Calculator, write the inputs down before looking at the result and independently check the units and direction of the calculation. A worked example is most useful when it shows not only the arithmetic but also why each input belongs in the formula. Change one assumption at a time and observe which output moves. That process helps distinguish a sensitive input from one that has little effect in the current scenario.","Do not copy an example's assumptions into a real decision merely because the numbers look similar. Examples simplify reality. Replace every example value with information that matches the actual property, tax situation, investment, employment arrangement or clinical measurement involved, and preserve the source of important inputs so the result can be reviewed later."]},
{heading:"Interpret ranges rather than false precision",text:["The Gold vs Stocks Calculator can calculate precise arithmetic, but many inputs are estimates or measurements with uncertainty. Reporting many decimal places can imply more certainty than the underlying data supports. Round the final result to a level that is meaningful for the decision and use scenarios when assumptions can reasonably vary.","Sensitivity analysis is especially valuable when a small input change produces a large change in the conclusion. In those cases, the correct takeaway may be that the decision is assumption-sensitive rather than that one scenario is definitively right."]},
{heading:"Should stock returns use the S&P 500?",text:["The S&P 500 is a common proxy for large U.S. equities, but it is not synonymous with all stocks. Global equities, small companies and value or growth segments can produce different histories.","Choose a benchmark that matches the portfolio being evaluated and use a total-return version when dividends are part of the investment result."]},
{heading:"What gold price should be used?",text:["Spot gold is a useful reference, but an investor may own an ETF, futures exposure, allocated bullion or coins. The realized return can differ because of fees, spreads, storage, financing or tracking differences.","Match the data series to the vehicle whenever possible. If using spot prices as a proxy, disclose costs that the series omits."]},
{heading:"Does gold always rise when stocks fall?",text:["No. Gold has sometimes diversified equity risk, but it can also decline during stock-market stress or remain weak for long periods. Correlation varies across regimes.","Portfolio stress tests should include periods when both assets disappoint. Diversification reduces dependence on one outcome; it does not guarantee that one holding will always offset another."]},
{heading:"How does rebalancing change results?",text:["A periodically rebalanced portfolio sells part of assets that have grown above target and buys assets that have fallen below target. This changes the return path compared with simply buying both assets and never trading.","Specify the rebalancing frequency and include transaction or tax effects if they are material. Backtests with different rebalancing rules should not be compared as though they used identical strategies."]},
]},
"cardiac-output-calculator":{title:"Cardiac Output Calculator",sections:[
{heading:"What cardiac output represents",text:["Cardiac output is the volume of blood pumped by the heart per unit of time, commonly expressed in liters per minute. In its basic form, cardiac output equals heart rate multiplied by stroke volume. The calculation connects how often the heart beats with how much blood is ejected on each beat.","The number is physiologic, not a standalone diagnosis. Body size, age, activity, temperature, medications, pregnancy, illness and measurement method can all affect what is expected for an individual."]},
{heading:"Cardiac index adjusts for body size",text:["A cardiac output that is appropriate for a large adult may be high for a much smaller person. Cardiac index addresses this by dividing cardiac output by body surface area, typically producing a value in liters per minute per square meter.","Body surface area itself is estimated from height and weight using a formula. Different BSA equations can produce slightly different values, so calculations should use the same convention as the clinical source when precision matters."]},
{heading:"Stroke volume links filling, contraction and resistance",text:["Stroke volume is influenced by preload, myocardial contractility and afterload. Heart rate and stroke volume can compensate for each other within limits, which means two people can have the same cardiac output through different physiologic combinations.","A calculator that multiplies heart rate by stroke volume does not explain why either input has changed. Clinical interpretation may require blood pressure, oxygenation, examination, imaging, laboratory data and the patient's overall condition."]},
{heading:"Measurement methods are not interchangeable",text:["Cardiac output can be estimated using several clinical techniques, including thermodilution, Fick-based calculations, echocardiographic methods and other hemodynamic technologies. Each method has assumptions and sources of error. A calculated value from entered heart rate and stroke volume is only as reliable as the stroke-volume measurement.","When tracking a patient over time, clinicians often care about trends measured under comparable conditions. Apparent changes may reflect a different method, loading conditions or technical variability rather than a true change in cardiac performance."]},
{heading:"The Fick principle uses oxygen consumption",text:["The Fick approach relates systemic oxygen consumption to the difference between arterial and venous oxygen content. Conceptually, if tissues consume a known amount of oxygen and the blood loses a measurable amount of oxygen while passing through the systemic circulation, flow can be inferred.","In practice, measured versus assumed oxygen consumption and the quality of blood samples can affect the estimate. This is not a calculation to recreate casually without appropriate clinical measurements."]},
{heading:"Normal ranges require context",text:["Reference ranges for cardiac output and cardiac index are useful orientation points, but a number just inside or outside a range cannot by itself establish normal or abnormal cardiovascular function. A physically active person, a febrile patient and a critically ill patient can have very different hemodynamic states.","Clinicians interpret output together with perfusion, blood pressure, filling pressures, lactate, urine output, mental status and other findings when relevant. The appropriate target can depend on the clinical situation."]},
{heading:"Heart rate increases do not guarantee higher output",text:["At first, increasing heart rate can increase cardiac output. At very high rates, however, shortened filling time may reduce stroke volume. Rhythm abnormalities can also impair filling or coordinated contraction. Therefore, extrapolating the simple formula outside measured inputs can be misleading.","Similarly, a low heart rate is not necessarily harmful if stroke volume and perfusion are adequate. The formula describes the product of two measurements; it does not define the optimal heart rate for a person."]},
{heading:"Use the tool for education and arithmetic",text:["This calculator is best used to check arithmetic from known clinical or educational inputs and to understand how heart rate, stroke volume and body size relate. It cannot diagnose heart failure, shock, valve disease or any other condition, and it cannot determine treatment.","If cardiac output is being discussed because someone is acutely unwell, clinical evaluation is more important than an online result. New chest pain, severe shortness of breath, fainting, confusion or other emergency symptoms require appropriate urgent medical attention rather than calculator-based interpretation."]},
{heading:"Units are a common source of calculation errors",text:["Heart rate is usually entered as beats per minute and stroke volume as milliliters per beat. Their product is milliliters per minute, which must be divided by 1,000 to express cardiac output in liters per minute. A missing conversion can make the answer one thousand times too large.","Keep units beside every input during manual calculations. For example, 70 beats per minute multiplied by 70 milliliters per beat equals 4,900 milliliters per minute, or 4.9 liters per minute."]},
{heading:"Changes in output should be interpreted with perfusion",text:["Cardiac output is one part of oxygen delivery. Hemoglobin concentration and arterial oxygen saturation also affect how much oxygen the blood can carry to tissues. A seemingly adequate flow does not guarantee adequate oxygen delivery in every clinical circumstance.","Likewise, organ perfusion depends on vascular tone and pressure as well as flow. This is why bedside assessment integrates multiple hemodynamic and clinical variables rather than treating cardiac output as an isolated target."]},
{heading:"A worked example makes the calculation easier to audit",text:["When using the Cardiac Output Calculator, write the inputs down before looking at the result and independently check the units and direction of the calculation. A worked example is most useful when it shows not only the arithmetic but also why each input belongs in the formula. Change one assumption at a time and observe which output moves. That process helps distinguish a sensitive input from one that has little effect in the current scenario.","Do not copy an example's assumptions into a real decision merely because the numbers look similar. Examples simplify reality. Replace every example value with information that matches the actual property, tax situation, investment, employment arrangement or clinical measurement involved, and preserve the source of important inputs so the result can be reviewed later."]},
{heading:"Interpret ranges rather than false precision",text:["The Cardiac Output Calculator can calculate precise arithmetic, but many inputs are estimates or measurements with uncertainty. Reporting many decimal places can imply more certainty than the underlying data supports. Round the final result to a level that is meaningful for the decision and use scenarios when assumptions can reasonably vary.","Sensitivity analysis is especially valuable when a small input change produces a large change in the conclusion. In those cases, the correct takeaway may be that the decision is assumption-sensitive rather than that one scenario is definitively right."]},
{heading:"How is stroke volume calculated from cardiac output?",text:["If cardiac output and heart rate are known, stroke volume can be rearranged from the basic equation: stroke volume equals cardiac output divided by heart rate, with units converted consistently. For example, liters per minute must be converted to milliliters per minute before obtaining milliliters per beat.","The rearranged equation is arithmetic only. A derived stroke volume inherits the uncertainty of the cardiac-output measurement and should not be treated as an independent measurement."]},
{heading:"What is the difference between cardiac output and ejection fraction?",text:["Cardiac output measures flow per minute, whereas ejection fraction describes the proportion of ventricular blood volume ejected with each contraction. They answer different physiologic questions and are not interchangeable.","A person can have an ejection fraction within a reference range yet have abnormal hemodynamics for other reasons, and reduced ejection fraction does not by itself specify the current cardiac output."]},
{heading:"Why can fever change cardiac output?",text:["Fever and other high-metabolic states can increase circulatory demand and heart rate, potentially changing cardiac output. Low-output states can arise through very different mechanisms, including impaired pumping or inadequate filling.","The clinical meaning therefore depends on the reason for measurement and the patient's overall condition, not just comparison with a generic resting range."]},
{heading:"What is systemic vascular resistance?",text:["Systemic vascular resistance describes opposition to blood flow in the systemic circulation and is related to pressure gradients and cardiac output. It is a different hemodynamic variable but is often considered alongside flow in clinical settings.","Because pressure, flow and resistance interact, changing one number cannot be interpreted safely without understanding the broader hemodynamic state."]},
]},
}

